WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:05.120
Leah: Earlier in this session we talked about
the different elements of software maintenance.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:10.620
Let’s now look at the ‘improvements’ that can
be made during maintenance, and the potential

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.860
negative consequences that this may have.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.309
Chris: Preventive software maintenance

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:20.580
As we have mentioned previously, ‘Preventive
Maintenance’ refers to software changes

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:25.299
carried out to futureproof a product and
prepare for any potential changes ahead.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:31.539
This includes making code easier to scale
or maintain and managing legacy content.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.099
It also covers finding and fixing
latent faults in the product before

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.370
they evolve into operational faults.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.610
Leah: ‘Preventive software maintenance’
mainly takes place behind the scenes

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.819
and relates more to tidying and
preparation, rather than major changes.

00:00:48.509 --> 00:00:53.579
Users of the software are unlikely to notice
preventive software changes directly, but

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.610
they may see the positive effect later.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.430
This is because preventive maintenance
can mean the smoother implementation of

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.740
large-scale changes further down the line.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:08.370
However, a negative consequence of this may
be the introduction of new bugs or errors that

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.410
may go undetected and affect functionality.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.039
Chris: Corrective software maintenance

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:19.169
Corrective changes in software maintenance
fix bugs and defects in the software.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.339
These are often carried out by implementing
quick, small updates as and when required.

00:01:25.289 --> 00:01:30.420
Users are less likely to give negative feedback
as they will be happy any issues or bugs are

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:36.610
fixed making their experience smoother, as well
as making a system or application more reliable.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:42.200
Leah: There may, however, be occasions when a
corrective fix may cause some disruption to users.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:47.379
Imagine if a bug or flaw in the software
has been present for some time and the user

00:01:47.389 --> 00:01:52.030
has grown used to its presence and therefore
adapted their behaviour to accommodate the flaw.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.660
Fixing the issue may mean that they
will again have to change their

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.890
behaviour leading to negative feedback.

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:03.349
A corrective change may upset a user if they
have problems with a certain bug or flaw

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.519
which isn’t fixed when other issues are.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:11.460
Chris: With corrective software maintenance,
it’s important to work fast and frequently which

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:16.859
will help to minimise any potential user problems
and keep your product working as expected.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.540
Leah: Adaptive software maintenance

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.809
The tech environment is constantly changing.

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:28.640
New knowledge, hardware, and cybersecurity threats
mean that software quickly becomes outdated.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.500
‘Adaptive software maintenance’
addresses this issue.

00:02:32.889 --> 00:02:36.240
Adaptive changes focus on the
infrastructure of the software.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.020
They’re made in response to new operating
systems, new hardware, and new platforms,

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.889
to keep the program compatible.

00:02:43.609 --> 00:02:48.679
Chris: Adaptive software changes tend to be
low impact for users as they deal with the

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.760
internal workings of the software such as
making sure it can integrate with new tech.

00:02:54.469 --> 00:02:59.910
Users may notice a small improvement in speed
or scalability but are otherwise unaffected.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:06.299
Users are, however, more likely to notice when
adaptive maintenance isn’t completed, as it

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:12.430
may mean their software stops working on their
devices or they encounter compatibility issues.

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.269
Leah: Perfective software maintenance

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.670
Perfective software maintenance is the
biggest category as it addresses the

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:22.450
functionality and usability of the software.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.779
It involves changing existing product
functionality by refining, deleting, or adding new

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:34.570
features and as well as changing the way a product
works, it might also change the way it looks.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:39.250
This may impact and change the user
interface or in-app user journey.

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:44.470
Chris: The scope and visibility of these changes
make perfective maintenance the most likely

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.919
to generate a negative response from users.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.990
Users won’t notice a bit of refactored
back-end code, but they will notice

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.160
any changes that are clearly visible in
the way the product looks and performs.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:02.980
Managing perfective changes means communicating
with your users to emphasis the positives and

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.430
mitigate any potential negative sentiment.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:11.540
This can be done by advertising, offering support
and responding effectively to product feedback.

